# xterm's stacks of video attributes (XTPUSHSGR CSI Pm # {, XTPOPSGR CSI # }, XTREPORTSGR
# CSI Pt ; Pl ; Pb ; Pr # |) and of colors (XTPUSHCOLORS CSI # P, XTPOPCOLORS CSI # Q, XTREPORTCOLORS
# CSI # R). The text is xterm's ctlseqs.

test a push and a pop with no parameters bring every attribute back
spec xterm ctlseqs XTPUSHSGR and XTPOPSGR: with no parameters all of the video attributes are saved, and popping restores them
size 10 4
feed \e[1;3;31m\e[#{\e[0;4;44m\e[#}x
cell 1,1 text=x attrs=bold,italic fg=idx:1 bg=default

test a push with parameters saves only what they name
spec xterm ctlseqs XTPUSHSGR: the optional parameters are the SGR codes of the attributes (1 bold, 3 italic, 30 foreground, 31 background, …); popping restores those that were pushed
size 10 4
feed \e[1;3;31m\e[1;30#{\e[0;4;32m\e[#}x
cell 1,1 text=x attrs=bold,underline fg=idx:1 bg=default

test the alias forms CSI # p and CSI # q push and pop
spec xterm ctlseqs: CSI # p is an alias for XTPUSHSGR (CSI # {), CSI # q for XTPOPSGR (CSI # })
size 10 4
feed \e[1m\e[#p\e[0m\e[#qx
cell 1,1 text=x attrs=bold

test a pop on an empty stack changes nothing
convention a terminal that keeps a stack has nothing to restore when it is empty; xterm leaves the rendition alone
size 10 4
feed \e[1m\e[#}x
cell 1,1 text=x attrs=bold

test the stack is ten deep
spec xterm ctlseqs XTPUSHSGR: the stack is limited to 10 levels
size 10 4
feed \e[38;5;1m\e[#{\e[38;5;2m\e[#{\e[38;5;3m\e[#{\e[38;5;4m\e[#{\e[38;5;5m\e[#{
feed \e[38;5;6m\e[#{\e[38;5;7m\e[#{\e[38;5;8m\e[#{\e[38;5;9m\e[#{\e[38;5;10m\e[#{
feed \e[38;5;11m\e[#{\e[0m
feed \e[#}\e[#}\e[#}\e[#}\e[#}\e[#}\e[#}\e[#}\e[#}\e[#}\e[#}x
cell 1,1 text=x fg=idx:1

test XTREPORTSGR answers the attributes every cell of the rectangle has
spec xterm ctlseqs XTREPORTSGR: the response is an SGR sequence which contains the attributes which are common to all cells in a rectangle
size 10 3
feed \e[1;3;31mab\e[0;1;4;31mcd\e[0m
feed \e[1;1;1;4#|
reply \e[0;1;31m
feed \e[1;1;1;1#|
reply \e[0;1;3;31m
feed \e[1;1;1;5#|
reply \e[0m

test XTPUSHCOLORS and XTPOPCOLORS put the palette back
spec xterm ctlseqs XTPUSHCOLORS and XTPOPCOLORS: push the dynamic and ANSI palette colors onto a stack, pop to set them back
size 10 4
feed \e]4;1;rgb:ff/00/00\a\e[#P\e]4;1;rgb:00/ff/00\a\e]4;1;?\a
reply \e]4;1;rgb:0000/ffff/0000\a
feed \e[#Q\e]4;1;?\a
reply \e]4;1;rgb:ffff/0000/0000\a

test XTPUSHCOLORS with a slot stores without pushing and XTPOPCOLORS restores without popping
spec xterm ctlseqs XTPUSHCOLORS and XTPOPCOLORS: parameters 1 through 10 store into, and restore from, a slot of the stack without pushing or popping
size 10 4
feed \e]10;rgb:11/22/33\a\e[1#P\e]10;rgb:44/55/66\a\e[1#Q\e]10;?\a
reply \e]10;rgb:1111/2222/3333\a
feed \e]10;rgb:77/88/99\a\e[1#Q\e]10;?\a
reply \e]10;rgb:1111/2222/3333\a

test XTREPORTCOLORS counts the stack
spec xterm ctlseqs XTREPORTCOLORS: report the current entry on the palette stack and the number of palettes stored, in the form CSI ? Pn ; Pm # Q
size 10 4
feed \e[#R
reply \e[?0;0#Q
feed \e[#P\e[#P\e[#R
reply \e[?2;2#Q
feed \e[#Q\e[#R
reply \e[?1;1#Q
